{"step_index":12,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T07:29:58Z","content":"Priyanshu,\n\nHere's the raw data — paper by paper.\n\n## 70-Mark Papers: Theory vs Numerical Breakdown\n\n| Paper | Numerical Marks (forced minimum) | Theory Marks | Numerical % |\n|---|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n| Summer 2026 | 7 (NPV or CUSUM) | 63 | 10% |\n| Summer 2025 | 7-14 (NPV/CUSUM forced + Heat exchanger optional) | 56-63 | 10-20% |\n| Summer 2024 | 7 (NPV or IRR) | 63 | 10% |\n| Summer 2023 | 7 (Boiler efficiency — both OR options numerical) | 63 | 10% |\n| Summer 2022 | **0** | **70** | **0%** |\n| Winter 2025 | **0** | **70** | **0%** |\n| Winter 2024 | **0** | **70** | **0%** |\n| Winter 2023 | 7 (Boiler efficiency — both OR options numerical) | 63 | 10% |\n| Winter 2022 | 11-22 (NPV forced + compressor/boiler forced) | 48-59 | 16-31% |\n\n**Average across 9 papers:** ~5-7 marks numerical per paper. **This subject is 90%+ theory.**\n\nThree papers (S22, W25, W24) had **zero numericals** — 100% theory.\n\n## Now the Critical Filter: Your 30-Mark Mid-Sem (Ch 1-3 Only)\n\nHere's what changes everything. Look at which numericals are actually IN your syllabus:\n\n| Numerical Type | Appears in PYQs | In Your Syllabus? |\n|---|:---:|:---:|\n| NPV calculation | 5 papers | ❌ OUT |\n| CUSUM calculation | 4 papers | ❌ OUT |\n| Boiler efficiency (indirect method) | 4 papers | ✅ IN |\n| Heat exchanger calculation | 1 paper | ❌ OUT |\n| IRR calculation | 1 paper | ❌ OUT |\n| Compressor comparison | 1 paper | ❌ OUT |\n| Lighting SPP/cashflow | 1 paper | ❌ OUT |\n\n**The only numerical that can appear in your mid-sem is boiler efficiency by indirect method.** Everything else is out of scope.\n\n## What to Expect in Your 30-Mark Paper\n\n| Type | Expected Marks | Confidence |\n|---|:---:|---|\n| Pure theory (define, explain, discuss, list, compare, short note) | **23-30 marks** | Very high — 3 out of 9 full papers had zero numericals |\n| Boiler efficiency numerical | **0 or 7 marks** | Medium — it appears in 4/9 papers, but GTU can easily make a 30-mark paper without it |\n\n**Bottom line:** Prepare for a paper that is **77-100% theory**. The boiler numerical is insurance — learn it if you have time, but don't lose sleep over it. Your marks will come from how well you can write 3-mark definitions, 4-mark explanations, and 7-mark \"discuss in detail\" answers on EC Act, BEE, Indian energy scenario, CDM, furnace measures, and steam traps.\n\nTheory writing quality is what decides your score here, not calculation speed.","thinking":"The user wants a breakdown of how much theory vs numerical marks are in each 70-mark paper, and then what they can expect in their 30-mark mid-sem. Let me go through all 9 papers and count theory vs numerical marks.\n\nLet me classify each question as Theory (T) or Numerical (N):\n\n**SUMMER 2026:**\n- Q1(a) Define: Energy Audit, Designated Consumers, ESCOs — 3m — T\n- Q1(b) Sector wise Indian energy scenario — 4m — T\n- Q1(c) CDM methodology and project cycle — 7m — T\n- Q2(a) List missions under NAPCC — 3m — T\n- Q2(b) Explain Blow down — 4m — T\n- Q2(c)/OR BEE schemes / EC Act features — 7m — T\n- Q3(a)/OR Objectives / definitions — 3m — T\n- Q3(b)/OR Energy conservation / audit instruments — 4m — T\n- Q3(c)/OR Energy audit steps / boiler ECOs — 7m — T\n- Q4(a)/OR Payback/ROI/NPV / EMIS — 3m — T\n- Q4(b)/OR M&T elements / cash flows — 4m — T\n- Q4(c)/OR NPV numerical / CUSUM numerical — 7m — N\n- Q5(a)/OR Deaeration / topping-bottoming cycles — 3m — T\n- Q5(b)/OR Steam traps / insulation thickness — 4m — T\n- Q5(c)/OR Furnace losses / WHR devices — 7m — T\n\nAlmost entirely theory across this paper, with numericals confined mainly to Q4(c) covering NPV and CUSUM analysis. Now let me work out the mandatory question path for this Summer 2026 paper.\n\nFor Summer 2026, only Q4(c)/Q4(c)OR is numerical at 7m, giving 7/70 = 10% numerical content, with the rest theory.\n\nFor Summer 2025, numerical options appear in Q3(c)/Q3(c)OR (NPV or CUSUM, both 7m numerical) and Q5(c) (Heat exchanger, 7m numerical, though its OR is theory-based Heat pipe). So depending on choices, numerical content ranges from a minimum of 7m (picking only Q3's numerical option) up to a maximum of 14m if both Q3 and Q5 numerical options are chosen.\n\nFor Summer 2024 and Summer 2023, both years follow the same pattern where Q4(c) and its OR are both numerical at 7m each, forcing 7/70 = 10% numerical regardless of choice.\n\nNow I'm looking at Summer 2022, where Q2(b) has a 4m NPV numerical and Q2(c) involves a 7m simple payback method that leans more toward theory with worked examples than pure calculation.\n\nActually, re-reading more carefully, both Q2(b) and Q2(c) appear to be theory questions explaining concepts rather than solving numerical problems, so I need to reconsider the classification for S22.\n\nChecking Q5(c) on CUSUM chart procedure — it's borderline, could involve calculation but reads more like a conceptual/diagram-based question. Still not finding a clear-cut numerical problem in S22.\n\nContinuing through Q4-Q5 covering dew point, HCV, cold storage, boilers, waste heat, cogeneration, ECBC, EC Act, CUSUM, and sustainable development — all theory questions. So S22 comes out to zero numerical marks out of 70, meaning this paper is entirely theory-based.\n\nNow checking Winter 2025: Q1 covers BEE schemes, energy sources, and sectoral consumption; Q2 covers EC Act features, EMIS, ECBC, and Electricity Act; Q3 covers energy audit instruments, CUSUM concepts, and definitions of SPP/ROI/NPV/IRR without actual calculations, plus ESCO roles and audit classification — everything so far is theory again.\n\nContinuing through, Q4 covers furnace, boiler efficiency methods, AC plants, steam pipe insulation, pinch analysis, and cogeneration versus trigeneration — all theory. Q5 covers PCF, UNFCCC, CDM, Bachat Lamp Yojna, Kyoto Protocol, and energy management practices — also entirely theory, meaning Winter 2025 has zero numerical questions out of 70 marks, 100% theory-based paper.\n\nNow checking Winter 2024: covers definitions, BEE schemes, EC Act 2001, NPV vs IRR comparison, M&T benefits, ESCOs, Indian energy scenario, energy management, refrigeration factors, audit instruments, M&T elements, audit definitions, preliminary vs detailed audits, and present value/NPV definitions — all theory-based so far, plus cold storage conservation questions.\n\nI notice Winter 2024 continues with thermic fluid heaters, dew point/HCV/latent heat, topping vs bottoming cycles, and repeats the earlier answer for one part. The remaining questions cover audit instruments again, furnace design, CDM, energy saving tips, steam trap installation, and Bachat Lamp Yojana — confirming this entire paper is 100% theory with zero numerical questions. Moving into Winter 2023, I'm seeing it start with energy source classification and ROI/NPV/IRR concepts.\n\nContinuing through Q4 and Q5 topics — ice bank systems, compressed air savings, cogeneration, boiler blowdown, and two boiler efficiency numericals worth 7 marks each in W23, giving a numerical weight of 10%. Then I'm moving into Winter 2022 starting with Q1(a) on energy.\n\nContinuing through Q4-Q5: coal vs oil boiler testing, compressor comparison numerical, cogeneration topping/bottoming, WHR development, boiler efficiency numerical, then steam condensate recovery, distribution precautions, Kyoto Protocol/CDM, CDM benefits, PCF mission, and furnace design parameters. I'm noting the W22 numerical questions are NPV (mandatory, 4m) and lighting system (7m, optional).\n\nNow I'm working out the marks arithmetic — figuring out the minimum guaranteed numerical marks (Q2b's 4m plus whichever compressor/boiler numerical is chosen for 7m gives 11m) versus the maximum possible if all optional numerical paths are chosen, which would total 22m depending on which OR branches are selected for Q2c and Q3b.\n\nFilling in the rest of the papers, numerical marks stay low across most sessions except W22, mostly hovering around 10% or zero, so overall the numerical component averages out to roughly 5-7 marks per paper across sessions.\n\nNow filtering by syllabus scope, only the boiler efficiency indirect method numerical actually falls within the 3-chapter syllabus for the mid-sem — everything else (NPV, IRR, CUSUM, heat exchanger, compressor, lighting) is excluded. So for this 30-mark exam, I'd expect only 0-7 marks of numerical content, with the remaining 23+ marks being theory-based."}
